The issue you're experiencing is due to the way JavaScript handles variable scope. In JavaScript, variables declared inside a function (i.e., local variables) have their own scope, which means they can be accessed from anywhere within that function. On the other hand, variables declared outside of functions (i.e., global variables) have only one scope: the global scope.

When you use `var` in your code, it creates a new scope for each declaration. This means that if you declare two variables with `var`, both will have their own scope. However, when you try to access these variables from another part of your code, JavaScript looks up the variables first based on their scope. If there's no matching scope, it defaults to the global scope.

Here's an example:

```javascript
// Global scope
console.log(a); // undefined

// Local scope
function foo() {
  console.log(b); // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'b' before initialization
}
foo();
console.log(a); // ReferenceError: Cannot access 'a' before initialization

// Global scope again
console.log(a); // 10

// Local scope again
foo();
console.log(a); // 10
```

In this case, `a` has its own scope, but `b` still has its own scope as well. When you try to access `b` from `foo`, JavaScript looks up `b` first, then tries to access `a`. Since `a` doesn't exist yet, it throws a ReferenceError.

To avoid this issue, consider using `let` or `const` instead of `var` for declaring variables. These keywords also create scopes, but they do so at the block level (`if/else` statements, loops, etc.) rather than the function level.

```javascript
// Block scope
console.log(a); // undefined

// Block scope again
if (condition) {
  let b = 10; // Block scope
} else {
  const c = 20; // Block scope
}
console.log(b); // 10
console.log(c); // 20
```

